
/* ===== materials.js ===== */

const MATERIALS = {"chapters":[{"id":1,"name":"Polymer foundations","level":"Foundation","description":"Build the molecular picture: chains, intermolecular forces, thermoplastics, thermosets and elastomers.","lessons":[["Macromolecules & chain architecture","A polymer is made from long-chain molecules. Chain length, branching, side groups and intermolecular forces help determine how the material flows, solidifies and performs.",["Molecular architecture affects mobility, entanglement and packing.","A polymer family contains many grades; molecular weight distribution and additives can make grades behave differently.","Injection-moulding behaviour is a consequence of molecular structure, not just the trade name."],"Compare two grades from the same polymer family. Note which datasheet properties differ and ask what molecular/additive differences could explain them.","Do not assume all grades of PP, PA, PC or any other family process or perform identically."],["Thermoplastics, thermosets & elastomers","Thermoplastics can be softened and reshaped by heat within their stability limits. Thermosets form irreversible networks during cure. Elastomeric behaviour is a mechanical response, not a single chemistry.",["Most conventional injection-moulding training focuses on thermoplastics, but thermoset and elastomer processes use different material chemistry and control logic.","Crosslinking changes the meaning of 'melt' and reprocessing.","TPE is a broad family; behaviour varies substantially by chemistry."],"Take three products and identify whether their material is likely thermoplastic, thermoset or elastomeric. Explain what that implies for re-melting.","Do not use thermoplastic processing assumptions for reactive thermoset systems."],["Molecular weight & distribution","Molecular weight and its distribution influence melt strength, viscosity and mechanical performance. Processing history can reduce molecular weight through degradation.",["Higher molecular weight often increases melt viscosity and entanglement, but grade design is more complex than one number.","MFR/MFI is only a single-point flow test and does not fully describe a material's rheology.","Hydrolysis or thermal degradation can reduce chain length and properties."],"Look at a resin datasheet that reports MFR/MVR. Identify the test temperature and load and explain why a value measured under another condition cannot be compared blindly.","Never translate MFR directly into a universal injection speed or pressure."],["Polymer families are not recipes","A family name identifies chemistry, not a guaranteed process window. Reinforcement, impact modification, flame retardants, colour, molecular weight and supplier formulation can alter behaviour.",["Grade-specific supplier documentation is the primary source for approved processing guidance.","Colour and additive packages can influence flow, shrinkage, thermal stability and appearance.","Validated production settings should be built around the actual grade and machine/mould combination."],"Choose one family you use at work and list at least three grade-level features that could make one version behave differently from another.","Do not teach a single melt temperature, drying temperature or shrinkage value as 'the value' for a polymer family."]]},{"id":2,"name":"Morphology & transitions","level":"Foundation → Intermediate","description":"Understand amorphous and semi-crystalline structure, Tg, Tm and crystallisation.","lessons":[["Amorphous vs semi-crystalline","Amorphous polymers lack long-range crystalline order. Semi-crystalline polymers contain crystalline regions together with an amorphous fraction.",["Morphology affects shrinkage, transparency, stiffness, chemical resistance and thermal behaviour.","Semi-crystalline materials commonly show greater moulding shrinkage because crystallisation increases packing density.","Cooling history can change the amount and structure of crystallinity."],"Classify common materials you use as amorphous or semi-crystalline, then compare observed shrinkage and visual appearance.","Semi-crystalline does not mean '100% crystalline'; the moulded part contains both crystalline and amorphous material."],["Glass transition, Tg","Tg is a transition associated with mobility in the amorphous phase. Below Tg the amorphous phase is more glassy; above Tg segmental mobility increases.",["Tg is not the same as crystalline melting.","Both amorphous polymers and the amorphous fraction of semi-crystalline polymers can exhibit Tg.","Service behaviour can change dramatically around Tg."],"Find Tg information for one material and relate it to the part's service-temperature requirement.","Do not call Tg the melting point of an amorphous polymer."],["Melting & crystallisation","Crystalline regions melt over a material-dependent range associated with Tm. During cooling, semi-crystalline materials may crystallise if chain mobility and time permit.",["Crystallisation kinetics depend on chemistry, nucleation, temperature history and additives.","Mould temperature and cooling history can affect crystallinity and therefore dimensions/properties.","A faster cooling history often reduces the opportunity for crystallisation, but the magnitude is material-specific."],"For a semi-crystalline resin, ask what changes when the mould runs colder or hotter while all other factors are held as constant as practical.","Do not assume 'hotter mould equals better' or 'colder mould equals faster' without checking quality, crystallisation and cycle effects."],["Why morphology matters to moulding","The morphology created during filling, packing and cooling becomes part of the component. It connects process history to dimensional and mechanical behaviour.",["Skin and core can cool at different rates.","Flow and cooling create orientation and morphology gradients through the wall.","Post-mould conditioning or annealing may change dimensions/properties for some materials."],"Take a warped or dimensionally unstable part and list which morphology-related mechanisms could contribute before changing settings.","Do not treat shrinkage as a single scalar material constant independent of flow, pressure, geometry and cooling."]]},{"id":3,"name":"Rheology & flow","level":"Intermediate","description":"Learn shear-thinning, temperature dependence, pressure loss, fountain flow and orientation.","lessons":[["Shear-thinning viscosity","Most thermoplastic melts are shear-thinning: apparent viscosity falls as shear rate increases. This is one reason injection speed changes pressure response.",["Viscosity depends on shear rate and temperature, not a single constant.","A speed change can alter pressure, shear heating and orientation at the same time.","Relative-viscosity studies are useful because they expose process sensitivity."],"Compare peak injection pressure and fill time at several controlled fill speeds while maintaining a consistent transfer condition.","Do not interpret one MFR/MFI value as the full viscosity curve."],["Temperature & melt viscosity","Within a material's safe processing range, higher melt temperature generally reduces melt viscosity. Excessive temperature or residence time can cause degradation.",["Temperature changes also alter cooling load and cycle behaviour.","Actual melt temperature can differ from barrel set temperatures.","The correct processing range is grade-specific."],"Compare a measured/verified melt-temperature condition with the supplier's grade guidance and current process record.","Do not raise temperature simply to make a difficult fill disappear; first identify the pressure-loss mechanism and material limits."],["Fountain flow & frozen layers","During cavity filling, hot melt advances through the interior of the flow and turns outward at the flow front, contacting the cooler mould wall and forming a progressively frozen skin.",["Fountain flow contributes to orientation patterns and surface formation.","Wall thickness, mould temperature, speed and material rheology affect the developing frozen layer.","Surface defects can originate from flow-front behaviour."],"Use a controlled short-shot sequence to infer the filling pattern and where flow fronts meet, under your site's authorised procedure.","A flow-front visual is conceptual; it is not a CFD result or permission to defeat normal guarding."],["Pressure loss & geometry","Pressure is consumed as melt moves through nozzle, sprue, runner, gate and cavity. Thin sections, small gates, long flow lengths and material viscosity can increase pressure demand.",["Machine pressure capability must exceed the process demand with appropriate margin.","A pressure-limited process can lose control of commanded velocity.","Tooling restrictions cannot always be corrected by machine settings."],"Map pressure demand through a controlled pressure-loss or short-shot study where appropriate.","Do not compensate indefinitely for a restrictive gate or damaged flow path by increasing pressure."]]},{"id":4,"name":"Thermal behaviour & cooling","level":"Intermediate","description":"Connect heat capacity, conductivity, cooling rate, crystallisation and ejection condition.","lessons":[["Heat in, heat out","The machine adds heat through heaters and mechanical work; the mould removes heat. The thermal balance determines melt condition, cooling time and part temperature.",["Plastics conduct heat relatively poorly compared with mould metals.","Thick sections cool disproportionately slowly.","Cooling-system performance depends on temperature, flow, circuit design and heat transfer."],"Trend cycle time and ejection condition while checking actual coolant temperatures and flow, not only controller setpoints.","Do not assume adding cooling time fixes the root cause of every dimensional problem."],["Why thickness dominates cooling","Heat must travel from the interior of the part to the mould surface. As wall thickness increases, the thermal path grows and cooling time increases strongly.",["Thick bosses and ribs can retain heat long after the skin looks solid.","Uneven section thickness promotes differential shrinkage.","Core temperature can remain high at ejection."],"Compare a thin wall and thick boss on the same part. Identify which region is likely to control safe ejection.","Surface appearance alone does not prove the centre of a thick section is cool."],["Mould temperature as a material variable","Mould temperature changes surface freezing, replication, crystallisation and cooling history. It is both a tooling and material-science variable.",["Amorphous and semi-crystalline materials can respond differently to mould temperature.","Higher mould temperature may improve surface replication or crystallisation but increase cooling demand.","Uniformity across the mould matters as much as the nominal setpoint."],"Measure or verify mould thermal balance at meaningful locations and compare with a known-good process.","Do not use one mould-temperature target for every grade or every quality requirement."],["Thermal degradation & residence history","Polymer can degrade through excessive heat, time, oxygen, shear, moisture-assisted reactions or incompatible contamination.",["Degradation may change colour, odour, viscosity and mechanical properties.","Residence time is a distribution, not a perfect single number.","Shutdown/start-up and small-shot-on-large-barrel conditions can increase thermal-history risk."],"For a degradation concern, review the complete material history: dryer, barrel, hot runner, recovery, interruptions and purge.","Do not 'cook out' a contamination or degradation problem by simply increasing temperature."]]},{"id":5,"name":"Moisture, drying & degradation","level":"Intermediate → Advanced","description":"Understand hygroscopicity, hydrolysis, drying variables, contamination and safe material handling.","lessons":[["Hygroscopic vs non-hygroscopic behaviour","Some polymers absorb moisture into the bulk material; others mainly carry surface moisture. The distinction matters because melt processing can magnify moisture-related defects or degradation.",["PA, PC, PET and PBT are examples where moisture control can be especially important, depending on grade and process.","A hopper that 'feels dry' is not a moisture measurement.","Supplier drying guidance is grade-specific."],"Identify which materials in your plant have documented pre-processing moisture requirements and how those are verified.","Do not classify a material as safely dry based only on storage time or appearance."],["Drying is a system","Effective drying depends on the resin, initial moisture, air condition, temperature, airflow, residence time, hopper loading and dryer performance.",["Dryer set temperature alone does not prove dry resin.","Dew point describes drying-air moisture, not the resin's actual moisture content.","Overdrying can also create problems for some materials/additives."],"Audit one drying system: material identity, dryer condition, air path, hopper loading, residence time and the method used to verify moisture where required.","Do not turn a supplier drying recommendation into a universal rule for all grades."],["Hydrolysis during melt processing","In hydrolysis-sensitive polymers, moisture at melt-processing temperature can break polymer chains and reduce molecular weight.",["The result may include reduced toughness/strength even if the part looks acceptable.","Splay can be a moisture clue but is not a complete moisture test.","Corrective action starts with material handling and verified drying, not cosmetic machine adjustments."],"If a hydrolysis-sensitive material shows unexpected brittleness, review moisture history alongside melt temperature and residence history.","Do not accept a cosmetically clean part as proof that hydrolytic degradation did not occur."],["Contamination & incompatible materials","Cross-contamination can create cosmetic, mechanical, rheological and sometimes serious thermal-decomposition hazards.",["Material-change procedures should reflect compatibility and supplier safety information.","Regrind identity and segregation are part of material control.","Purging compounds and cleaning methods have their own safety requirements."],"Trace a material path from storage to hopper and identify every point where another resin, colour or regrind stream could enter.","Do not mix unknown polymers or purge materials; follow the relevant SDS, supplier and site procedure."]]},{"id":6,"name":"Mechanical & long-term properties","level":"Advanced","description":"Interpret stiffness, strength, toughness, creep, fatigue, impact and environmental effects.","lessons":[["Stress–strain behaviour","Stress–strain curves describe how a material responds to loading. Modulus, yield, elongation and fracture behaviour capture different aspects of performance.",["A stiff material is not automatically a tough material.","Test speed, temperature, specimen geometry and conditioning affect results.","Moulding orientation can make the part response differ from a standard test specimen."],"Compare modulus, tensile strength and elongation for two candidate grades and explain why no single number defines 'stronger'.","Do not rank materials using tensile strength alone."],["Creep & stress relaxation","Polymers can continue deforming under constant load (creep) and lose stress under fixed deformation (stress relaxation). Time and temperature matter.",["Long-term design may be controlled by creep rather than short-term tensile strength.","Higher temperature often accelerates viscoelastic deformation.","Moisture conditioning can change behaviour for some polymers."],"For a clip, snap fit or loaded boss, ask whether the design requirement is short-term strength or long-term load retention.","Do not use a short tensile test as the only evidence for long-term dimensional stability."],["Impact, notches & weld lines","Impact performance depends on material toughness, temperature, strain rate, notches, weld lines, orientation and residual stress.",["A sharp corner can dominate failure even when the bulk material is tough.","Weld-line strength can differ from the surrounding material.","Conditioning and fibre orientation can change impact behaviour."],"Inspect the fracture origin of a failed part and relate it to geometry, weld lines, gate position and moulding orientation.","Do not assume a material's catalogue impact value applies unchanged to the moulded component."],["Fatigue & cyclic loading","Repeated loading can produce failure at stresses below a material's one-time strength. Cycles, mean stress, environment and geometry matter.",["Living hinges are a specialised fatigue application associated with suitable material/geometry combinations.","Filled materials can have different fatigue mechanisms from unfilled grades.","Surface damage and stress concentrators shorten fatigue life."],"Identify components that flex or vibrate repeatedly and classify them as fatigue-critical rather than only strength-critical.","Do not validate a cyclic application using one static pull test."]]},{"id":7,"name":"Shrinkage, orientation & warpage","level":"Advanced","description":"Connect volumetric shrinkage, packing, cooling, molecular/fibre orientation and anisotropy.","lessons":[["Volumetric shrinkage","Polymer density changes as the melt cools and solidifies. Packing pressure can compensate part of this volume change while pressure can still transmit through the gate.",["Gate seal limits how long packing can influence the cavity.","Semi-crystalline morphology adds another source of density change.","Local thickness and cooling affect how shrinkage appears."],"Relate a gate-seal/part-mass study to sink, dimensions and packing effectiveness.","Do not keep adding hold time after the gate has stopped transmitting pressure."],["Molecular orientation","Flow stretches and orients polymer molecules. Some orientation is frozen into the part as the material cools.",["Orientation can create direction-dependent shrinkage and mechanical behaviour.","Higher shear and rapid freezing can increase retained orientation in some conditions.","Relaxation depends on temperature and time before solidification."],"Use gate position and flow direction to predict where orientation-driven dimensional effects may appear.","Do not treat a moulded polymer as isotropic by default."],["Fibre orientation & anisotropy","Short fibres tend to orient with local flow, creating strong direction-dependent stiffness, strength and shrinkage.",["Gate location and flow pattern strongly influence fibre orientation.","Weld lines can interrupt fibre continuity.","A fibre-filled grade can reduce some shrinkage while increasing directional differences."],"Sketch the likely principal flow directions in a glass-fibre-filled part and compare them with observed warpage or stiffness.","Do not apply unfilled-resin shrinkage assumptions to a fibre-filled grade."],["Warpage is a system response","Warpage develops when shrinkage, cooling, orientation or packing are non-uniform through the component.",["Thermal imbalance, geometry and fibre orientation can interact.","A setting change can improve one dimension while worsening another.","Tooling, material and process must be considered together."],"Build a cause map for a warped part using material, flow/orientation, packing, thermal balance and geometry before changing the recipe.","Do not troubleshoot warpage by changing mould temperature or hold pressure in isolation without evidence."]]},{"id":8,"name":"Fillers, fibres, additives & recyclate","level":"Advanced","description":"Learn how reinforcement, fillers, impact modifiers, colourants, flame retardants and recycled content change behaviour.","lessons":[["Glass fibre reinforcement","Glass fibres commonly increase stiffness and reduce thermal expansion, but they also change flow, wear, surface appearance and anisotropy.",["Fibre length can be reduced by processing history and high shear.","Orientation can dominate directional properties.","Abrasive reinforcement affects screw, barrel, nozzle, gate and tool wear."],"Compare a filled and unfilled grade from the same polymer family and identify changes in shrinkage, stiffness and processing notes.","Do not assume more fibre always means a better part."],["Mineral fillers & dimensional control","Mineral fillers can alter stiffness, shrinkage, density, surface behaviour, thermal conductivity and cost.",["Effects depend on filler type, shape, loading and interface with the polymer.","Filled compounds may respond differently to weld lines and impact.","Higher density changes shot-volume relationships."],"When changing to a filled grade, list which machine, tool and quality checks should be repeated rather than copying the old recipe.","Do not assume a filled material can use the same shot-volume and pressure behaviour as the unfilled grade."],["Additives, colour & flame retardants","Additives can change stabilisation, UV resistance, lubricity, impact, flame response, colour and processing behaviour.",["Colour masterbatch carrier compatibility matters.","Flame-retardant packages can change thermal stability and corrosion concerns.","Supplier-approved let-down ratios and compatibility should be followed."],"For a colour or additive change, identify which quality characteristics and process outputs should be rechecked.","Do not treat masterbatch as inert pigment only."],["Regrind & recycled content","Regrind and recyclate carry additional history: heat exposure, contamination opportunity, moisture, colour variation and possible molecular-weight change.",["Control starts with identity, segregation and known history.","Repeated processing can change properties depending on polymer and stabilisation.","Regulated or critical applications may have strict material-control requirements."],"Map how regrind is generated, identified, stored, blended and traced in one process.","Do not add an uncontrolled regrind percentage simply to reduce scrap."]]},{"id":9,"name":"Material selection & data interpretation","level":"Advanced → Expert","description":"Use requirements, datasheets, test standards, processing needs and risk to choose a material intelligently.","lessons":[["Translate the product requirement","Material selection starts with the component's loads, temperature, environment, life, tolerance, appearance, regulatory needs and manufacturing process.",["A material cannot be selected from strength alone.","Chemical exposure, UV, flame, food/medical contact and electrical requirements can be decisive.","Geometry and moulding process affect whether datasheet performance is achievable."],"Write a material-selection requirement for one real component before naming any polymer family.","Do not begin with 'we always use this material' unless the requirements justify it."],["Reading a technical data sheet","A TDS reports properties under stated test methods and conditioning. Numbers are only meaningful when you understand how they were measured.",["Check units, test standard, specimen state and conditioning.","Typical values are not automatically specification limits.","Processing recommendations can be grade-specific and may be revised by the supplier."],"Pick five TDS properties and write the test method/condition beside each value.","Do not compare two numbers from different test methods as if they are directly equivalent."],["ISO vs ASTM and test-method awareness","ISO and ASTM test methods can use different specimen geometries, speeds, conditioning and calculations. Similar property names do not guarantee interchangeable values.",["The test method is part of the property definition.","Conversion between unit systems does not remove method differences.","Component testing is often required even when material data looks favourable."],"Find one property reported using different test standards and list the method differences you would need to understand before comparing.","Do not treat a converted unit as proof that two test results are equivalent."],["Material approval & change control","A material change can affect processing, dimensions, appearance, mechanical performance, compliance and validation status.",["Supplier, grade, colour, additive or recycled-content changes may require review.","Critical applications need documented approval criteria.","A successful short run does not automatically prove long-term equivalence."],"Build a change-control checklist for moving from one approved grade to an alternative.","Do not substitute a 'similar' resin in production without the required technical and quality approval."]]}],"families":[{"name":"PP","full":"Polypropylene","morph":"Semi-crystalline","moisture":"Low","shrink":80,"chemical":85,"impact":65,"stiff":55,"optical":35,"notes":"Lightweight family with strong chemical resistance and excellent hinge-fatigue potential in suitable grades/designs. Shrinkage and orientation require attention.","tags":["chemical resistance","living hinge","low density"]},{"name":"HDPE","full":"High-density polyethylene","morph":"Semi-crystalline","moisture":"Low","shrink":90,"chemical":90,"impact":75,"stiff":45,"optical":20,"notes":"Tough, chemically resistant family with relatively high shrinkage and creep considerations.","tags":["chemical resistance","toughness","low density"]},{"name":"ABS","full":"Acrylonitrile butadiene styrene","morph":"Amorphous","moisture":"Medium","shrink":35,"chemical":45,"impact":80,"stiff":60,"optical":20,"notes":"Balanced impact and surface-quality family. Grade-specific drying and thermal-history guidance should be followed.","tags":["impact","surface finish","amorphous"]},{"name":"PC","full":"Polycarbonate","morph":"Amorphous","moisture":"High","shrink":30,"chemical":40,"impact":95,"stiff":70,"optical":95,"notes":"High toughness and optical capability in suitable grades. Moisture and thermal history are important because hydrolytic/thermal degradation can reduce properties.","tags":["impact","transparent","high performance"]},{"name":"PA","full":"Polyamide / Nylon","morph":"Semi-crystalline","moisture":"High","shrink":65,"chemical":75,"impact":75,"stiff":70,"optical":20,"notes":"Strong engineering family whose dimensions and mechanical properties can change with moisture conditioning. Drying and post-mould condition matter.","tags":["engineering","wear","hygroscopic"]},{"name":"POM","full":"Acetal / Polyoxymethylene","morph":"Semi-crystalline","moisture":"Low","shrink":75,"chemical":80,"impact":65,"stiff":75,"optical":15,"notes":"Low-friction, dimensionally capable engineering family. Strict supplier thermal-stability and contamination guidance is important.","tags":["low friction","precision","wear"]},{"name":"PBT","full":"Polybutylene terephthalate","morph":"Semi-crystalline","moisture":"High","shrink":60,"chemical":75,"impact":55,"stiff":75,"optical":15,"notes":"Engineering polyester family used widely in electrical and dimensionally controlled parts. Moisture control before melt processing is important.","tags":["electrical","engineering","hydrolysis sensitive"]},{"name":"PET","full":"Polyethylene terephthalate","morph":"Semi-crystalline capable","moisture":"High","shrink":55,"chemical":70,"impact":55,"stiff":75,"optical":70,"notes":"Polyester family where drying and crystallisation history are central. Final morphology depends strongly on grade and cooling history.","tags":["polyester","hydrolysis sensitive","crystallisation"]},{"name":"PMMA","full":"Acrylic / Polymethyl methacrylate","morph":"Amorphous","moisture":"Medium","shrink":30,"chemical":45,"impact":35,"stiff":70,"optical":100,"notes":"Optical clarity and surface quality are key strengths. Notch sensitivity and grade-specific drying/handling matter.","tags":["transparent","optical","surface"]},{"name":"TPE","full":"Thermoplastic elastomer family","morph":"Varies by chemistry","moisture":"Varies","shrink":50,"chemical":55,"impact":85,"stiff":20,"optical":45,"notes":"A broad family rather than one material. Hardness, bonding, morphology, drying and processing differ substantially among TPE chemistries.","tags":["flexible","soft touch","broad family"]}],"quiz":[["Which statement best describes a semi-crystalline thermoplastic?",["It contains crystalline regions together with an amorphous fraction","Its solid structure is essentially fully crystalline once normal mould cooling is complete","Its amorphous fraction is negligible, so glass-transition behaviour is not relevant","Its crystalline fraction is fixed by chemistry and is effectively independent of thermal history"],0,"Semi-crystalline thermoplastics contain both crystalline and amorphous regions. The balance and morphology depend on chemistry, additives, pressure and thermal history; the amorphous fraction can still exhibit a glass transition."],["What does Tg describe most accurately?",["The temperature range where crystalline lamellae melt","The glass transition associated with increased segmental mobility in the amorphous phase","The temperature at which crystallisation proceeds fastest during mould cooling","The temperature below which packing pressure can no longer affect the cavity"],1,"Tg concerns segmental mobility in the amorphous phase. It is distinct from crystalline melting (Tm), crystallisation kinetics and gate/solidification behaviour."],["Which statement about Tm is most accurate?",["It is the same transition as Tg but measured during heating","It is the minimum mould temperature needed to produce crystallinity","It is associated with melting of crystalline regions","It is normally the preferred injection-moulding melt-temperature setpoint"],2,"Tm is associated with melting crystalline regions. A processing temperature is grade- and process-specific and must not be inferred from Tm alone."],["Why should MFR/MFI not be treated as a complete description of injection-moulding viscosity?",["It measures flow only after the polymer has crystallised","It captures the full processing shear-rate range but not temperature dependence","It can be converted directly into viscosity if the machine screw diameter is known","It is an empirical flow test under specified temperature/load conditions and does not reproduce the full processing rheology"],3,"MFR/MFI is valuable for specified-condition comparison and quality control, but it is not a full rheological curve. Processing shear rates, temperature, pressure and material history matter."],["For most thermoplastic melts, increasing shear rate generally causes apparent viscosity to:",["Fall because of shear-thinning behaviour","Rise because molecular chains increasingly resist deformation at higher shear rate","Remain approximately constant unless the melt temperature also changes","Change only if the material contains glass fibre or mineral filler"],0,"Most thermoplastic melts are shear-thinning over relevant processing ranges. The magnitude depends on material and temperature."],["Within a resin's approved processing range, increasing melt temperature will generally:",["Reduce viscosity and therefore automatically reduce total cycle time","Reduce melt viscosity while also changing cooling demand and degradation risk","Have little effect on viscosity but mainly change packing pressure","Reduce viscosity enough that moisture history is no longer important"],1,"Higher melt temperature generally lowers melt viscosity, but it also changes the thermal load and can increase degradation risk. It does not remove moisture or other material-history requirements."],["What is fountain flow?",["A near-plug-flow condition where the material touching the mould wall continues travelling fastest","A recirculating flow that develops behind the screw during plasticising","The advancing-flow behaviour in which interior melt moves toward the front and turns outward toward the mould wall","The flow pattern produced only when a hot-runner valve gate opens"],2,"Fountain flow is the characteristic behaviour at the advancing cavity flow front. It contributes to skin/core structure and orientation."],["Why can a small gate or thin section raise injection-pressure demand?",["A reduced section always increases polymer density enough to require more pressure","A smaller flow area lowers velocity, so extra pressure is required to maintain the same fill time","The effect is mainly caused by clamp force acting against the melt path","Restricted geometry can increase viscous resistance and pressure loss through the flow path"],3,"Thin sections and restrictive gates can raise viscous pressure loss. The actual response depends on flow rate, temperature, geometry and rheology."],["What is the most accurate statement about moulding shrinkage?",["It is influenced by material, pressure history, morphology/orientation, geometry and cooling","The supplier's nominal shrinkage range is normally sufficient to predict every finished-part dimension","Once gate seal has occurred, final shrinkage is governed almost entirely by mould temperature","Sufficient holding pressure can eliminate material shrinkage if it is applied for long enough"],0,"Moulding shrinkage is a coupled material/process/geometry response. Datasheet ranges are useful design inputs, not universal part predictions."],["Why can glass-fibre-filled parts be strongly anisotropic?",["The fibre primarily changes crystallinity, while mechanical properties remain nearly direction-independent","Fibres orient with local flow, creating direction-dependent stiffness, strength and shrinkage","Fibre orientation is usually random after the melt passes the gate, so anisotropy comes mainly from cooling","Anisotropy occurs only when the glass-fibre content is above a single critical percentage"],1,"Short fibres can align strongly with local flow and through-thickness flow history, producing directional mechanical and shrinkage behaviour."],["Which statement about moisture-sensitive polymers is best?",["Following a family-level drying-temperature table is normally sufficient if residence time is long enough","A low dryer dew point proves the pellets have reached the required moisture content","Moisture control should follow grade-specific supplier guidance and, where required, verified resin moisture","Absence of visible splay is sufficient evidence that hydrolysis-sensitive material is dry enough"],2,"Drying is a system. Grade, starting moisture, air condition, airflow, residence time and verification method all matter; visual appearance alone is not proof."],["What does dryer dew point tell you?",["The moisture content of the resin once it has remained in the hopper for the specified time","The maximum temperature at which the dryer can safely operate","The equilibrium moisture content the polymer will have after moulding","The moisture condition of the drying air, not directly the resin's actual moisture content"],3,"Dryer dew point characterises the drying air. Pellet moisture still depends on the complete drying system, starting condition and material behaviour."],["What can hydrolysis do to a susceptible polymer during melt processing?",["Break susceptible polymer chains and reduce molecular weight","Temporarily plasticise the melt while leaving molecular weight unchanged after cooling","Increase molecular weight through moisture-assisted chain extension","Affect crystallisation only, without materially changing chain integrity"],0,"Hydrolysis can cause chain scission in susceptible polymers at processing temperature, reducing molecular weight and potentially mechanical performance."],["Why can a part look acceptable yet still have material degradation?",["Thermal degradation is expected to show a visible surface change before properties are affected","Cosmetic appearance does not directly measure molecular weight or mechanical integrity","A smooth or glossy surface is reliable evidence that hydrolysis and oxidation are controlled","A stable viscosity result rules out degradation mechanisms that mainly affect mechanical properties"],1,"Some degradation mechanisms can reduce molecular weight or properties without an obvious cosmetic signature. Use material history and appropriate testing/evidence."],["Which statement about stress–strain data is most accurate?",["Tensile strength is generally enough to rank materials if the specimens use the same nominal polymer family","Modulus and tensile strength together fully describe impact and long-term behaviour","Modulus, yield, elongation and fracture describe different aspects of behaviour, and the test conditions matter","Conditioning affects measured properties only when the material is visibly moisture-sensitive"],2,"Mechanical behaviour cannot be reduced to one number. Test method, specimen, rate, temperature and conditioning are part of the result."],["Creep is best described as:",["Permanent deformation that begins when stress exceeds yield","Loss of stress with time while a specimen is held at fixed strain","Progressive damage caused by repeated cyclic loading","Time-dependent deformation under sustained load"],3,"Creep is time-dependent deformation under sustained load. Stress relaxation and fatigue are related time-dependent phenomena but are not the same definition."],["Why can weld lines reduce component performance?",["Flow fronts can meet with different thermal, molecular/fibre orientation and bonding histories, creating a locally different region","A weld line is normally only cosmetic if the cavity completely fills","Packing pressure after transfer restores molecular/fibre continuity to the same state as uninterrupted flow","Weld-line strength is mainly a fixed material property independent of venting, temperature and flow history"],0,"Weld-line performance depends on how the fronts meet: temperature, pressure, venting, contamination, molecular diffusion and fibre continuity can all matter."],["What is the strongest way to approach warpage?",["Start with packing-pressure history because it is normally the dominant warpage mechanism","Treat it as a combined material, orientation, packing, cooling, geometry and tooling problem","Treat mould-temperature imbalance as the primary mechanism until it is disproved","Use additional cooling time as the first test before checking orientation or cavity balance"],1,"Warpage is a system response. The best diagnosis starts with competing mechanisms and evidence, then uses controlled tests."],["Why can repeated reprocessing of regrind matter?",["If the resin identity is unchanged, repeated heat history has little relevance to final properties","Drying the regrind to the approved condition resets its previous thermal and shear history","Repeated processing can add thermal/shear history and change molecular weight, contamination risk or additive condition","The important variable is regrind colour consistency; molecular history is normally secondary"],2,"Regrind carries a history. The significance depends on polymer, stabilisation, application, contamination control and the number/severity of process histories."],["What should happen before substituting a 'similar' resin grade in a validated production process?",["Matching the polymer family, colour and nominal shrinkage is generally sufficient evidence","Matching MFR/MVR and the main tensile properties is normally sufficient for a like-for-like substitution","A successful short run with acceptable dimensions is sufficient if the machine recipe is unchanged","Review technical equivalence and follow the required material/change-control approval"],3,"A grade change can affect processing, dimensions, durability, compliance and validation status. The required technical/quality change-control process must decide equivalence."],["Why can two datasheet tensile-strength values be unsafe to compare directly?",["Test standard, specimen geometry, conditioning and test rate may differ","Values in MPa and psi cannot be converted accurately enough for engineering comparison","Different polymer families require tensile strength to be measured at the same elongation","Datasheet tensile values are comparable whenever both suppliers call the property 'tensile strength'"],0,"The test method and specimen condition are part of the property definition. Unit conversion alone does not make unlike methods equivalent."],["Which statement about masterbatch/additives is best?",["Once the carrier is compatible, additives affect appearance but rarely process rheology or long-term properties","Carrier, compatibility, concentration and additive package can influence processing and final properties","The base resin's approved process window automatically covers any colour or additive package","Supplier approval is mainly a purchasing requirement rather than a technical material-control issue"],1,"Colourants and additives can affect viscosity, stability, crystallisation, weathering, flame response, appearance and mechanical behaviour."],["A semi-crystalline polymer is cooled more rapidly. What is the safest general statement?",["A faster cooling rate will always produce lower final crystallinity regardless of material, thickness or later thermal history","Cooling rate mainly changes crystal size but not crystallisation temperature or degree","It often reduces the time available for crystallisation, but the actual morphology depends on material kinetics and the full thermal history","Cooling-rate effects become negligible once a polymer is classified as semi-crystalline"],2,"For many semi-crystalline systems faster cooling reduces crystallisation opportunity, but kinetics, mesophase formation, nucleation and later thermal history can change the outcome."],["What is residual molecular orientation?",["A temperature gradient that remains in the part after the mould opens","The preferred direction of fibre reinforcement only; unfilled polymers do not retain flow-related orientation","The direction in which the screw last rotated before decompression","Flow-induced molecular orientation that remains partly frozen into the solid part"],3,"Polymer molecules can be oriented by flow and some orientation can be retained as the material cools, contributing to directional properties and shrinkage."],["Why is a fibre-filled grade likely to wear processing equipment more than an unfilled grade?",["Many reinforcing fibres/fillers are abrasive and can increase wear in the melt path and tooling","The main wear increase comes from the higher density forcing the screw to contact the barrel more strongly","Fibre-filled materials generally require higher clamp force, which transfers wear directly to the screw and nozzle","Wear is mainly caused by fibre-induced changes in moulding shrinkage rather than abrasion"],0,"Many reinforcing fillers are abrasive. Wear also depends on filler type/loading, fibre length, processing conditions and equipment materials/design."],["What is the best first step in material selection?",["Start with the strongest resin family, then check whether its cost and processability are acceptable","Define component loads, environment, temperature, life, geometry, regulatory and manufacturing requirements","Start with the lowest-cost candidate that meets the nominal operating temperature","Choose the resin already used in the most similar-looking component and validate it later"],1,"Good material selection begins with the complete requirement. Only then should candidate families/grades be compared and validated."],["Which statement best captures good material-science practice in injection moulding?",["Use family-level tendencies as target values, then tune the machine until the part meets appearance requirements","Once the machine recipe repeats, material history becomes a secondary variable unless there is visible degradation","Combine polymer science with grade-specific supplier data, measured process evidence and component requirements","Treat a supplier TDS as a finished-part guarantee provided the moulding process stays within the listed processing range"],2,"Sound practice connects general polymer science to the actual grade, its history, the process, tooling, product requirements and appropriate verification."]]};
let materialTab="learn";
let materialLessonId=1;

function ensureMaterialState(){
  user.materialScience=user.materialScience||{};
  const m=user.materialScience;
  if(!Array.isArray(m.completed))m.completed=[];
  if(m.bestQuiz==null)m.bestQuiz=null;
  if(!m.quizAttempts)m.quizAttempts=0;
  if(!m.currentLesson)m.currentLesson=1;
  return m;
}
function materialLessons(){
  const out=[];let id=1;
  for(const c of MATERIALS.chapters){
    for(const l of c.lessons){
      out.push({id:id++,chapter:c.id,chapterName:c.name,level:c.level,title:l[0],intro:l[1],points:l[2],exercise:l[3],trap:l[4]});
    }
  }
  return out;
}
const MAT_LESSONS=materialLessons();
function materialProgress(){
  const m=ensureMaterialState();
  return Math.round(m.completed.length/MAT_LESSONS.length*100);
}
function matChapterProgress(c){
  const m=ensureMaterialState();
  const ids=MAT_LESSONS.filter(x=>x.chapter===c.id).map(x=>x.id);
  const done=ids.filter(id=>m.completed.includes(id)).length;
  return {done,total:ids.length,pct:Math.round(done/ids.length*100)};
}
function switchMaterialTab(tab){
  materialTab=tab;renderMaterials();setTimeout(initMaterialLabs,0);window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"});
}
function materialTabsHTML(){
  return `<div class="mat-tabs">
    <button class="${materialTab==="learn"?"active":""}" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('learn')">Learn</button>
    <button class="${materialTab==="explorer"?"active":""}" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('explorer')">Material explorer</button>
    <button class="${materialTab==="labs"?"active":""}" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('labs')">Interactive labs</button>
    <button class="${materialTab==="quiz"?"active":""}" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('quiz')">Knowledge check</button>
  </div>`;
}
function renderMaterials(){
  const root=$("#materials");if(!root)return;
  ensureMaterialState();
  if(materialTab==="learn")root.innerHTML=renderMaterialLearn();
  if(materialTab==="explorer")root.innerHTML=renderMaterialExplorer();
  if(materialTab==="labs")root.innerHTML=renderMaterialLabs();
  if(materialTab==="quiz")root.innerHTML=renderMaterialQuiz();
}
function renderMaterialLearn(){
  const m=ensureMaterialState(),pct=materialProgress();
  return `<div class="mat-shell">
    <div class="card mat-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow">Material Science · Injection Moulding</span>
      <h2>Understand what the polymer is doing — not just what the machine is set to.</h2>
      <p>Learn structure, morphology, rheology, heat transfer, moisture, degradation, mechanical behaviour, shrinkage, fibre orientation, additives, recyclate and material selection. The content deliberately avoids universal temperature/drying recipes because those belong to the actual resin grade and supplier documentation.</p>
      <div class="hero-buttons"><button class="primary" data-mm-onclick="openMaterialLesson(${m.currentLesson||1})">Continue material science →</button><button class="ghost" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('labs')">Try an interactive lab</button></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-kpis">
      <div class="card mat-kpi"><span>Material lessons</span><strong>${MAT_LESSONS.length}</strong></div>
      <div class="card mat-kpi"><span>Chapters</span><strong>${MATERIALS.chapters.length}</strong></div>
      <div class="card mat-kpi"><span>Progress</span><strong>${pct}%</strong></div>
      <div class="card mat-kpi"><span>Best material quiz</span><strong>${m.bestQuiz==null?"—":m.bestQuiz+"%"}</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-disclaimer"><b>Important:</b> Family-level statements in this module describe general tendencies. Actual properties, drying requirements, processing limits, shrinkage and compliance depend on the specific grade, additives, conditioning, test method, machine, mould and part. Use the resin supplier's current TDS/SDS and your approved process for production decisions.</div>
    <div class="section-head"><div><h2>9 material-science chapters</h2><p>From molecules to real component behaviour.</p></div></div>
    <div class="mat-chapters">${MATERIALS.chapters.map(c=>{
      const p=matChapterProgress(c);
      return `<div class="card mat-chapter"><span class="eyebrow">${esc(c.level)}</span><h3>${c.id}. ${esc(c.name)}</h3><p>${esc(c.description)}</p><div class="mini-bar"><span style="width:${p.pct}%"></span></div><div class="course-bottom"><span class="pill">${p.done}/${p.total} complete</span><button class="secondary" data-mm-onclick="openMaterialChapter(${c.id})">${p.pct?"Continue":"Start"}</button></div></div>`;
    }).join("")}</div>
  </div>`;
}
function openMaterialChapter(id){
  const first=MAT_LESSONS.find(x=>x.chapter===id&&!ensureMaterialState().completed.includes(x.id))||MAT_LESSONS.find(x=>x.chapter===id);
  openMaterialLesson(first.id);
}
function openMaterialLesson(id){
  materialLessonId=id;ensureMaterialState().currentLesson=id;persist();
  const l=MAT_LESSONS.find(x=>x.id===id),c=MATERIALS.chapters.find(x=>x.id===l.chapter);
  $("#materials").innerHTML=`${materialTabsHTML()}<div class="mat-learn-layout">
    <aside class="card mat-side"><span class="eyebrow">Chapter ${c.id}</span><h3 style="margin:6px 0">${esc(c.name)}</h3><div class="mini-bar"><span style="width:${matChapterProgress(c).pct}%"></span></div><div class="mat-lesson-list">${MAT_LESSONS.filter(x=>x.chapter===c.id).map(x=>`<button class="${x.id===l.id?"active":""}" data-mm-onclick="openMaterialLesson(${x.id})">${ensureMaterialState().completed.includes(x.id)?"✓ ":""}${esc(x.title)}</button>`).join("")}</div><button class="ghost" style="width:100%;margin-top:10px" data-mm-onclick="switchMaterialTab('learn')">← All chapters</button></aside>
    <article class="card mat-lesson">
      <span class="eyebrow">${esc(l.level)} · Material lesson ${l.id}/${MAT_LESSONS.length}</span><h2>${esc(l.title)}</h2>
      <p>${esc(l.intro)}</p>
      <h3>What matters</h3><ul>${l.points.map(x=>`<li>${esc(x)}</li>`).join("")}</ul>
      <div class="mat-evidence"><b>Evidence exercise:</b><br>${esc(l.exercise)}</div>
      <div class="mat-trap"><b>Common trap:</b><br>${esc(l.trap)}</div>
      <div class="mat-disclaimer"><b>Production boundary:</b> apply this science using the actual grade's current supplier documentation, your machine/mould limits, site procedures and approved quality requirements.</div>
      <div class="lesson-actions-sticky">
        ${l.id>1?`<button class="ghost" data-mm-onclick="openMaterialLesson(${l.id-1})">← Previous</button>`:""}
        <button class="primary" data-mm-onclick="completeMaterialLesson(${l.id})">${ensureMaterialState().completed.includes(l.id)?"Next lesson →":"Complete & continue →"}</button>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>`;
}
function completeMaterialLesson(id){
  const m=ensureMaterialState(),wasNew=!m.completed.includes(id);
  if(wasNew)m.completed.push(id);
  if(id<MAT_LESSONS.length)m.currentLesson=id+1;
  persist();
  if(wasNew&&typeof awardXP==="function")awardXP(20,"material-lesson-"+id,"Material science lesson",{celebrate:id%4===0});
  checkMaterialAchievements();
  if(id<MAT_LESSONS.length)openMaterialLesson(id+1);else{switchMaterialTab("quiz");toast("Material Science learning path complete")}
}

function renderMaterialExplorer(){
  return `<div class="mat-shell">${materialTabsHTML()}
    <div class="card mat-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Material explorer</span><h2>Compare polymer-family tendencies.</h2><p>Use this as a learning map, not as a material-approval tool. Ratings are deliberately relative and qualitative because actual grades vary widely.</p></div>
    <div class="mat-disclaimer"><b>Do not select a production material from this screen alone.</b> Confirm the actual grade against product requirements, supplier TDS/SDS, applicable regulations and component testing.</div>
    <div class="mat-explorer-controls">
      <label>Morphology<select id="matMorph" data-mm-onchange="filterMaterialExplorer()"><option value="">Any</option><option>Amorphous</option><option>Semi-crystalline</option></select></label>
      <label>Moisture sensitivity<select id="matMoist" data-mm-onchange="filterMaterialExplorer()"><option value="">Any</option><option>Low</option><option>Medium</option><option>High</option></select></label>
      <label>Need<select id="matNeed" data-mm-onchange="filterMaterialExplorer()"><option value="">Any</option><option value="impact">Higher impact tendency</option><option value="optical">Optical/clarity tendency</option><option value="chemical">Chemical-resistance tendency</option><option value="stiff">Higher stiffness tendency</option></select></label>
      <label>Search<input id="matSearch" placeholder="PP, nylon, optical..." data-mm-oninput="filterMaterialExplorer()"></label>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-material-grid" id="matMaterialGrid">${MATERIALS.families.map(materialCard).join("")}</div>
  </div>`;
}
function materialCard(m){
  const morph=m.morph.includes("Amorphous")?"Amorphous":m.morph.includes("Semi")?"Semi-crystalline":"Varies";
  return `<div class="card mat-material" data-morph="${esc(morph)}" data-moist="${esc(m.moisture)}" data-search="${esc((m.name+" "+m.full+" "+m.tags.join(" ")+" "+m.notes).toLowerCase())}" data-impact="${m.impact}" data-optical="${m.optical}" data-chemical="${m.chemical}" data-stiff="${m.stiff}">
    <span class="eyebrow">${esc(m.morph)}</span><h3>${esc(m.name)} · ${esc(m.full)}</h3><div class="mat-tags">${m.tags.map(t=>`<span class="mat-tag">${esc(t)}</span>`).join("")}</div>
    ${propMeter("Relative shrinkage tendency",m.shrink)}${propMeter("Chemical resistance tendency",m.chemical)}${propMeter("Impact tendency",m.impact)}${propMeter("Stiffness tendency",m.stiff)}${propMeter("Optical tendency",m.optical)}
    <p class="muted tiny" style="line-height:1.45">${esc(m.notes)}</p><div class="mat-ref">Moisture sensitivity: ${esc(m.moisture)} · qualitative family tendency only</div>
  </div>`;
}
function propMeter(label,v){return `<div class="mat-prop"><span>${label}</span><div class="mat-meter"><span style="width:${v}%"></span></div></div>`}
function filterMaterialExplorer(){
  const morph=$("#matMorph")?.value||"",moist=$("#matMoist")?.value||"",need=$("#matNeed")?.value||"",q=($("#matSearch")?.value||"").toLowerCase();
  $$("#matMaterialGrid .mat-material").forEach(el=>{
    let ok=(!morph||el.dataset.morph===morph)&&(!moist||el.dataset.moist===moist)&&(!q||el.dataset.search.includes(q));
    if(need)ok=ok&&+(el.dataset[need]||0)>=70;
    el.classList.toggle("hidden",!ok);
  });
}

function renderMaterialLabs(){
  return `<div class="mat-shell">${materialTabsHTML()}
    <div class="card mat-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Interactive material labs</span><h2>Experiment with concepts without turning them into production recipes.</h2><p>Every output is a relative teaching model. No lab gives an approved melt temperature, drying setpoint, pressure or material limit.</p></div>
    <div class="mat-labs">
      <div class="card mat-lab"><span class="eyebrow">Lab 1</span><h3>Rheology: shear-thinning</h3><p>Change relative shear rate, melt-temperature offset and filler tendency. Watch the illustrative viscosity index move.</p>
        <label>Relative shear rate<input id="rheoShear" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="45" data-mm-oninput="updateRheologyLab()"></label>
        <label>Relative melt-temperature offset<input id="rheoTemp" type="range" min="-20" max="20" value="0" data-mm-oninput="updateRheologyLab()"></label>
        <label>Relative filler effect<input id="rheoFill" type="range" min="0" max="40" value="0" data-mm-oninput="updateRheologyLab()"></label>
        <div class="mat-curve" id="rheoCurve"></div><div class="mat-lab-output" id="rheoOut"></div></div>

      <div class="card mat-lab"><span class="eyebrow">Lab 2</span><h3>Crystallisation tendency</h3><p>Conceptual semi-crystalline model: change cooling severity and relative mould thermal level.</p>
        <label>Cooling severity<input id="crCool" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" data-mm-oninput="updateCrystalLab()"></label>
        <label>Relative mould thermal level<input id="crMould" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" data-mm-oninput="updateCrystalLab()"></label>
        <div class="mat-lab-output" id="crystalOut"></div></div>

      <div class="card mat-lab"><span class="eyebrow">Lab 3</span><h3>Fibre orientation</h3><p>Visualise why a filled part can behave differently along and across the main flow direction.</p>
        <label>Orientation tendency<input id="fibOrient" type="range" min="10" max="100" value="65" data-mm-oninput="updateFibreLab()"></label>
        <div class="mat-arrow-field" id="fibreField"></div><div class="mat-lab-output" id="fibreOut"></div></div>

      <div class="card mat-lab"><span class="eyebrow">Lab 4</span><h3>Moisture-control reasoning</h3><p>Select a family and see the correct learning priority. No generic drying temperatures are provided.</p>
        <label>Material family<select id="dryFamily" data-mm-onchange="updateDryLab()">${MATERIALS.families.map(m=>`<option value="${esc(m.name)}">${esc(m.name)} · ${esc(m.full)}</option>`).join("")}</select></label>
        <div class="mat-lab-output" id="dryOut"></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-disclaimer"><b>Lab boundary:</b> these models demonstrate direction-of-effect and reasoning only. Real resin behaviour is nonlinear and grade-specific. Production parameters must come from the actual grade's supplier guidance and validated process development.</div>
  </div>`;
}
function updateRheologyLab(){
  const shear=+$("#rheoShear").value,temp=+$("#rheoTemp").value,fill=+$("#rheoFill").value;
  const idx=Math.max(25,Math.min(220,100*Math.exp(-0.010*shear)*Math.exp(-0.025*temp)*(1+fill/90)));
  const label=idx<60?"lower":idx>125?"higher":"moderate";
  $("#rheoOut").innerHTML=`Illustrative apparent-viscosity index: <b>${idx.toFixed(0)}</b> (${label}).<br><small>Direction taught: shear-thinning tends to lower apparent viscosity as shear rate rises; higher melt temperature tends to lower viscosity within the grade's safe range; fillers may raise viscosity and change shear response.</small>`;
  const pts=[];for(let x=0;x<=100;x+=5){const y=160-(110*Math.exp(-0.018*x)*(1+fill/120));pts.push(`${20+x*3.2},${Math.max(20,Math.min(180,y-temp*.8))}`)}
  $("#rheoCurve").innerHTML=`<svg viewBox="0 0 360 210"><line x1="20" y1="185" x2="345" y2="185" stroke="#516b8c"/><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="20" y2="185" stroke="#516b8c"/><polyline points="${pts.join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="#55d6be" stroke-width="4"/><text x="220" y="202" fill="#9fb4cf" font-size="11">relative shear rate →</text><text x="4" y="18" fill="#9fb4cf" font-size="11">relative viscosity</text></svg>`;
}
function updateCrystalLab(){
  const cool=+$("#crCool").value,mould=+$("#crMould").value;
  const tendency=Math.max(0,Math.min(100,55 + (mould-50)*.55 - (cool-50)*.5));
  const desc=tendency<35?"lower crystallisation opportunity":tendency>65?"higher crystallisation opportunity":"intermediate crystallisation opportunity";
  $("#crystalOut").innerHTML=`Conceptual result: <b>${desc}</b>.<br><small>For many semi-crystalline polymers, thermal history changes the time available for crystallisation. Actual kinetics may behave very differently by chemistry, nucleation and grade.</small>`;
}
function updateFibreLab(){
  const v=+$("#fibOrient").value;
  let lines="";for(let i=0;i<18;i++){const y=18+i*8;const angle=(Math.random()-.5)*(100-v)*.5;lines+=`<line x1="25" y1="${y}" x2="255" y2="${y+angle}" stroke="#68a7ff" stroke-width="3" opacity=".75"/>`}
  $("#fibreField").innerHTML=`<svg viewBox="0 0 280 180">${lines}<text x="82" y="170" fill="#9fb4cf" font-size="11">principal flow direction →</text></svg>`;
  $("#fibreOut").innerHTML=`Orientation tendency: <b>${v<40?"low-to-moderate":v<75?"moderate-to-high":"high"}</b>.<br><small>Higher alignment can increase directional stiffness/strength differences and directional shrinkage. Real orientation depends on gate, geometry, flow history and fibre aspect ratio.</small>`;
}
function updateDryLab(){
  const m=MATERIALS.families.find(x=>x.name===$("#dryFamily").value);
  let msg="";
  if(m.moisture==="High")msg=`<b>High moisture-control priority.</b> Confirm sealed handling, dryer performance, supplier-specified drying conditions and—where required—the approved resin-moisture measurement before processing.`;
  else if(m.moisture==="Medium")msg=`<b>Grade/process dependent moisture-control priority.</b> Check the actual grade's TDS/SDS and storage history; do not assume ambient storage is acceptable.`;
  else msg=`<b>Lower bulk hygroscopicity tendency, but moisture/condensation and contamination still matter.</b> Follow supplier storage and processing instructions for the actual grade.`;
  $("#dryOut").innerHTML=`${msg}<br><br><small>${esc(m.notes)}</small>`;
}
function initMaterialLabs(){if(materialTab==="labs"){updateRheologyLab();updateCrystalLab();updateFibreLab();updateDryLab()}}

let materialQuizOrder=[];
function shuffleMaterial(a){a=[...a];for(let i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]}return a}
function renderMaterialQuiz(){
  const m=ensureMaterialState();
  materialQuizOrder=shuffleMaterial(MATERIALS.quiz.map((q,i)=>({q,i}))).slice(0,18);
  return `<div class="mat-shell">${materialTabsHTML()}
    <div class="card mat-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Material Science knowledge check</span><h2>Test understanding, not memorised recipes.</h2><p>18 questions are selected from the material-science bank. The bank focuses on mechanisms, evidence and safe use of grade-specific supplier information.</p></div>
    <div class="mat-disclaimer"><b>Learning assessment only:</b> this material-science score does not replace the regional safety-gated certification exams.</div>
    <div class="card mat-quiz-card"><div id="materialQuizQuestions">${materialQuizOrder.map((item,qi)=>{
      const q=item.q;const opts=shuffleMaterial(q[1].map((text,idx)=>({text,idx})));
      return `<div class="mat-q" data-source="${item.i}"><b>${qi+1}. ${esc(q[0])}</b>${opts.map(o=>`<label><input type="radio" name="mq${qi}" value="${o.idx}"> ${esc(o.text)}</label>`).join("")}</div>`;
    }).join("")}</div><button class="primary" data-mm-onclick="gradeMaterialQuiz()">Grade & review</button><div id="materialQuizResult" class="callout hidden"></div><div id="materialQuizReview"></div></div>
  </div>`;
}
function gradeMaterialQuiz(){
  let correct=0;const rows=[];
  materialQuizOrder.forEach((item,qi)=>{
    const q=item.q,r=document.querySelector(`input[name=mq${qi}]:checked`),sel=r?+r.value:null,ok=sel===q[2];if(ok)correct++;
    rows.push(`<div class="mat-review ${ok?"correct":"incorrect"}"><b>${qi+1}. ${ok?"Correct ✓":"Review"}</b><br><span class="tiny">Correct answer: ${esc(q[1][q[2]])}</span><p class="muted" style="margin:7px 0 0">${esc(q[3])}</p></div>`);
  });
  const pct=Math.round(correct/materialQuizOrder.length*100),m=ensureMaterialState();m.quizAttempts++;m.bestQuiz=Math.max(m.bestQuiz||0,pct);persist();
  const box=$("#materialQuizResult");box.classList.remove("hidden");box.innerHTML=`<b>${correct}/${materialQuizOrder.length} correct — ${pct}%</b><br>${pct>=80?"Strong material-science result. Keep connecting the theory to the actual resin grade and process evidence.":"Review the explanations below and revisit the relevant chapters."}`;
  $("#materialQuizReview").innerHTML=rows.join("");
  if(pct>=80&&typeof awardXP==="function")awardXP(120,"material-quiz-80","Material Science mastery",{celebrate:true});
  checkMaterialAchievements();
}
function checkMaterialAchievements(){
  if(typeof FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS==="undefined")return;
  checkAchievements();
}
if(typeof FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS!=="undefined"){
  if(!FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.some(a=>a.id==="material-first"))FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.push({id:"material-first",icon:"🧬",name:"Molecule Mapper",desc:"Complete your first material-science lesson.",test:()=>ensureMaterialState().completed.length>=1});
  if(!FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.some(a=>a.id==="material-half"))FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.push({id:"material-half",icon:"🔬",name:"Polymer Investigator",desc:"Complete 18 material-science lessons.",test:()=>ensureMaterialState().completed.length>=18});
  if(!FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.some(a=>a.id==="material-master"))FUN_ACHIEVEMENTS.push({id:"material-master",icon:"⬡",name:"Material Science Master",desc:"Complete all 36 material-science lessons and score at least 80% on the material quiz.",test:()=>ensureMaterialState().completed.length>=36&&(ensureMaterialState().bestQuiz||0)>=80});
}

/* Route the new section without disturbing the hardened base application. */
const materialBaseSwitchView=switchView;
switchView=function(id){
  if(id!=="materials")return materialBaseSwitchView(id);
  currentView="materials";
  $$(".view").forEach(v=>v.classList.add("hidden"));
  $("#materials").classList.remove("hidden");
  $$("#nav button").forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle("active",b.dataset.view==="materials"));
  $$(".mobile-nav button[data-view]").forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle("active",b.dataset.view==="materials"));
  $("#pageTitle").textContent="Material Science";
  $("#pageSubtitle").textContent="Understand polymer behaviour from molecular structure to moulded-part performance.";
  renderMaterials();setTimeout(initMaterialLabs,0);window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"});
};

/* Dashboard entry point. */
const materialBaseDashboard=renderDashboard;
renderDashboard=function(){
  materialBaseDashboard();
  const grid=$("#dashboard .quick-grid");
  if(grid&&!grid.querySelector("[data-material-action]")){
    grid.insertAdjacentHTML("beforeend",`<button class="quick-action" data-material-action data-mm-onclick="switchView('materials')"><span class="icon">🧬</span><b>Explore material science</b><small>Polymers, rheology, moisture, crystallisation, properties and material selection.</small></button>`);
  }
};

/* Search material-science lessons as well as the existing platform. */
const materialBaseDoSearch=doSearch;
doSearch=function(){
  materialBaseDoSearch();
  const q=($("#globalSearch")?.value||"").toLowerCase().trim();if(q.length<2)return;
  const hits=MAT_LESSONS.filter(l=>(l.title+" "+l.intro+" "+l.points.join(" ")).toLowerCase().includes(q)).slice(0,6);
  if(hits.length&&$("#searchResults"))$("#searchResults").insertAdjacentHTML("beforeend",hits.map(l=>`<button class="search-item" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('materials');openMaterialLesson(${l.id})"><b>Material science:</b> ${esc(l.title)}<br><span class="muted tiny">${esc(l.chapterName)}</span></button>`).join(""));
};

/* Add material science to the mobile More menu. */
openMobileMenu=function(){openModal(`<span class="eyebrow">More</span><h2>Tools & progress</h2><div class="grid2">
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('materials')"><span class="icon">🧬</span><b>Material science</b><small>Polymer behaviour and interactive labs.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('simulator')"><span class="icon">⚙</span><b>Process simulator</b><small>Relative training model.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('defects')"><span class="icon">◇</span><b>Defect finder</b><small>Mechanisms and evidence checks.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('coach')"><span class="icon">✦</span><b>Troubleshooting coach</b><small>Structured offline guidance.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('exams')"><span class="icon">✓</span><b>Knowledge checks</b><small>Safety-gated assessments.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('standards')"><span class="icon">§</span><b>Standards & safety</b><small>UK, US and NZ references.</small></button>
  <button class="quick-action" data-mm-onclick="closeModal();switchView('profile')"><span class="icon">⚙</span><b>Profile & data</b><small>Preferences and backup.</small></button>
</div>`)}

/* Material-science progress in profile. */
const materialBaseProfile=renderProfile;
renderProfile=function(){
  materialBaseProfile();
  const m=ensureMaterialState();
  const head=$("#profile .section-head");
  if(head)head.insertAdjacentHTML("beforebegin",`<div class="card form-card" style="margin-top:14px"><span class="eyebrow">Material Science</span><h2>${materialProgress()}% complete</h2><p class="muted">${m.completed.length}/${MAT_LESSONS.length} lessons · Best knowledge check: ${m.bestQuiz==null?"—":m.bestQuiz+"%"}</p><div class="mini-bar"><span style="width:${materialProgress()}%"></span></div><button class="secondary" style="margin-top:12px" data-mm-onclick="switchView('materials')">Open material science</button></div>`);
};

ensureMaterialState();
renderDashboard();

